INSERT INTO topic_sections (topic_id, title, content, section_type, order_index) VALUES

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
'Concept Overview: Sequences and Series',
E'## What Are Sequences and Series?\n\nA **sequence** is an ordered list of numbers following a pattern. Each number is called a **term**.\nA **series** is the sum of the terms of a sequence.\n\nExample:\nSequence: 2, 5, 8, 11, 14, ...\nSeries: 2 + 5 + 8 + 11 + 14 + ...\n\nIn WASSCE, this topic carries 6-10 marks. The two most important types are:\n1. **Arithmetic Progressions (AP)** — constant difference between terms\n2. **Geometric Progressions (GP)** — constant ratio between terms\n\n### Why Sequences and Series Matter\n\n- **Finance**: Compound interest uses geometric sequences\n- **Physics**: Radioactive decay follows geometric sequences\n- **Architecture**: Repeating patterns use arithmetic sequences\n- **Computer science**: Algorithms often analyse sequences\n- **Population models**: Growth can be modelled as sequences\n\n### Key Questions WASSCE Asks\n\n1. Find the nth term of an AP or GP\n2. Find the sum of the first n terms\n3. Find a specific term given conditions\n4. Determine if a given series converges (GP with |r| < 1)\n5. Find the sum to infinity of a converging GP\n6. Use arithmetic mean and geometric mean',
'overview', 1),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
'Definitions & Key Terminologies',
E'## All Essential Terms and Formulas\n\n### Arithmetic Progression (AP)\n\nA sequence where each term is obtained by adding a constant value to the previous term.\n\n**Common difference (d)**: d = T2 - T1 = T3 - T2 = ... (constant)\n\nGeneral term: **Tn = a + (n-1)d**\n\nWhere:\n- a = first term\n- d = common difference\n- n = position of term\n- Tn = nth term\n\nSum of first n terms:\n**Sn = (n/2)[2a + (n-1)d]** or **Sn = (n/2)(a + l)**\nWhere l = last term = a + (n-1)d\n\n**Arithmetic Mean**: The term between two terms a and b in an AP is (a+b)/2.\n\n### Geometric Progression (GP)\n\nA sequence where each term is obtained by multiplying the previous term by a constant value.\n\n**Common ratio (r)**: r = T2/T1 = T3/T2 = ... (constant)\n\nGeneral term: **Tn = ar^(n-1)**\n\nSum of first n terms:\n- If r is not 1: **Sn = a(1 - r^n)/(1 - r)** OR **Sn = a(r^n - 1)/(r - 1)**\n- If r = 1: Sn = na\n\nFirst form is preferred when |r| < 1 (fraction).\nSecond form when r > 1.\n\n**Sum to Infinity (convergent GP)**: When |r| < 1:\n**S_inf = a/(1 - r)**\n\n**Geometric Mean**: The term between a and b in a GP is sqrt(ab).\n\n### Key Vocabulary\n\n**Term**: Each individual number in a sequence\n**First term**: The starting value (denoted a or T1)\n**Common difference**: The constant added in an AP (d)\n**Common ratio**: The constant multiplied in a GP (r)\n**Convergent series**: A GP with |r| < 1 that has a finite sum to infinity\n**Divergent series**: A series with no finite sum\n**Partial sum**: Sum of first n terms (Sn)\n**Arithmetic mean**: Midpoint of two terms in AP context\n**Sigma notation**: Sum(k=1 to n) of Tk = T1 + T2 + ... + Tn',
'definitions', 2),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
'Deep Explanation: Sequences and Series Methods',
E'## Complete Method Guides\n\n### Part 1: Identifying AP vs GP\n\nGiven a sequence, first determine which type it is:\n\n**For AP**: Check if T2 - T1 = T3 - T2 = T4 - T3 (differences are equal)\n**For GP**: Check if T2/T1 = T3/T2 = T4/T3 (ratios are equal)\n\nSequence: 3, 6, 12, 24, ...\nDifferences: 3, 6, 12 — NOT equal, so NOT AP\nRatios: 6/3 = 2, 12/6 = 2, 24/12 = 2 — equal, so GP with r = 2\n\nSequence: 7, 11, 15, 19, ...\nDifferences: 4, 4, 4 — equal, so AP with d = 4\n\n### Part 2: Finding Terms in AP\n\n**Find the nth term**: Use Tn = a + (n-1)d\n\nExample: AP with a = 5, d = 3. Find T10.\nT10 = 5 + (10-1)(3) = 5 + 27 = 32\n\n**Find n given a term value**: Set up equation and solve.\nExample: When does 5 + 3(n-1) = 50?\n3(n-1) = 45\nn-1 = 15\nn = 16 (the 16th term is 50)\n\n**Finding a and d given two terms**:\nIf T3 = 11 and T7 = 23:\nT3 = a + 2d = 11 ...(i)\nT7 = a + 6d = 23 ...(ii)\nSubtract (i) from (ii): 4d = 12, d = 3\nFrom (i): a = 11 - 6 = 5\n\n### Part 3: Sum of AP\n\nSn = (n/2)[2a + (n-1)d]\n\nExample: Find the sum of the first 20 terms of 3 + 7 + 11 + ...\na = 3, d = 4, n = 20\nS20 = (20/2)[2(3) + (20-1)(4)]\n= 10[6 + 76]\n= 10 x 82 = 820\n\nAlternatively, find the 20th term and use Sn = (n/2)(a + l):\nT20 = 3 + 19(4) = 79\nS20 = (20/2)(3 + 79) = 10 x 82 = 820\n\n### Part 4: Finding Terms in GP\n\n**Find the nth term**: Tn = ar^(n-1)\n\nExample: GP with a = 2, r = 3. Find T5.\nT5 = 2 x 3^(5-1) = 2 x 81 = 162\n\n**Finding a and r given two terms**:\nIf T2 = 6 and T5 = 48:\nT2 = ar = 6 ...(i)\nT5 = ar^4 = 48 ...(ii)\nDivide (ii) by (i): r^3 = 8, r = 2\nFrom (i): a = 6/2 = 3\n\n### Part 5: Sum of GP\n\nSn = a(1 - r^n)/(1 - r) when r < 1\nSn = a(r^n - 1)/(r - 1) when r > 1\n\nExample: Find the sum of 1 + 3 + 9 + 27 + ... (8 terms)\na = 1, r = 3, n = 8\nS8 = 1(3^8 - 1)/(3-1) = (6561-1)/2 = 6560/2 = 3280\n\n### Part 6: Sum to Infinity of GP\n\nCondition: The GP converges only when |r| < 1 (i.e., -1 < r < 1).\nIf |r| >= 1, the sum to infinity does not exist.\n\nS_inf = a/(1-r)\n\nExample: 4 + 2 + 1 + 1/2 + ...\na = 4, r = 1/2 (|r| < 1, so it converges)\nS_inf = 4/(1 - 1/2) = 4/(1/2) = 8\n\n**Application**: Converting recurring decimals\n0.333... = 0.3 + 0.03 + 0.003 + ...\na = 0.3, r = 0.1\nS_inf = 0.3/(1-0.1) = 0.3/0.9 = 1/3 ✓',
'explanation', 3),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
'Worked Examples: Sequences and Series',
E'## Eight Worked Examples (Basic to Exam-Standard)\n\n### Example 1 — Basic: The first term of an AP is 4 and the common difference is 6. Find the 15th term.\n\nTn = a + (n-1)d\nT15 = 4 + (15-1)(6)\n= 4 + 14 x 6\n= 4 + 84 = 88\n\n**Answer: T15 = 88**\n\n---\n\n### Example 2 — Basic: A GP has first term 5 and common ratio 2. Find the sum of the first 6 terms.\n\nS6 = a(r^n - 1)/(r - 1) = 5(2^6 - 1)/(2-1) = 5(64-1)/1 = 5 x 63 = 315\n\n**Answer: S6 = 315**\n\n---\n\n### Example 3 — Intermediate: The 4th term of an AP is 16 and the 10th term is 46. Find (a) the common difference, (b) the first term, (c) the 20th term.\n\nT4 = a + 3d = 16 ...(i)\nT10 = a + 9d = 46 ...(ii)\nSubtract (i) from (ii): 6d = 30, d = 5\nFrom (i): a = 16 - 15 = 1\nT20 = 1 + 19(5) = 1 + 95 = 96\n\n**Answers: d = 5, a = 1, T20 = 96**\n\n---\n\n### Example 4 — Intermediate: Find the sum of all even numbers from 2 to 100.\n\nSequence: 2, 4, 6, ..., 100 — this is an AP\na = 2, d = 2, last term l = 100\nFind n: Tn = a + (n-1)d\n100 = 2 + (n-1)(2)\n98 = 2(n-1)\nn-1 = 49\nn = 50\n\nS50 = (50/2)(2 + 100) = 25 x 102 = 2550\n\n**Answer: Sum = 2550**\n\n---\n\n### Example 5 — Intermediate: A GP has 2nd term 12 and 4th term 108. Find (a) common ratio and first term, (b) sum of first 5 terms.\n\n(a) T2 = ar = 12 ...(i)\nT4 = ar^3 = 108 ...(ii)\nDivide (ii) by (i): r^2 = 9, r = 3 (taking positive root)\nFrom (i): a = 12/3 = 4\n\n(b) S5 = 4(3^5 - 1)/(3-1) = 4(243-1)/2 = 4 x 242/2 = 4 x 121 = 484\n\n**Answers: r = 3, a = 4, S5 = 484**\n\n---\n\n### Example 6 — Challenging: Find the sum to infinity of 18 - 6 + 2 - 2/3 + ...\n\nCheck ratio: r = -6/18 = -1/3; |r| = 1/3 < 1, so sum to infinity exists.\n\nS_inf = a/(1-r) = 18/(1-(-1/3)) = 18/(1+1/3) = 18/(4/3) = 18 x 3/4 = 54/4 = 13.5\n\n**Answer: S_inf = 13.5**\n\n---\n\n### Example 7 — Challenging: How many terms of the AP 5 + 8 + 11 + ... are needed for the sum to exceed 400?\n\na = 5, d = 3\nSn = (n/2)[2(5) + (n-1)(3)] = (n/2)[10 + 3n - 3] = (n/2)(3n + 7)\n\nSet Sn > 400:\n(n/2)(3n + 7) > 400\nn(3n + 7) > 800\n3n^2 + 7n - 800 > 0\n\nUsing quadratic formula: n = [-7 + sqrt(49 + 9600)]/(6) = [-7 + sqrt(9649)]/6\nsqrt(9649) approx 98.23\nn > (-7 + 98.23)/6 = 91.23/6 approx 15.2\n\nSo n = 16 (first whole number exceeding 15.2)\nVerify: S16 = (16/2)(3x16 + 7) = 8(55) = 440 > 400 ✓\nS15 = (15/2)(3x15 + 7) = (15/2)(52) = 390 < 400 ✓\n\n**Answer: 16 terms are needed**\n\n---\n\n### Example 8 — Exam Standard: An AP has first term a and common difference d. Given that T3 = 9 and S5 = 35, find the values of a and d. Hence find the product of the 3rd and 5th terms.\n\nT3 = a + 2d = 9 ...(i)\nS5 = (5/2)[2a + 4d] = 35\n5(a + 2d) = 35\na + 2d = 7 ...(ii)\n\nBut equations (i) and (ii) are identical! This means only one solution exists.\nFrom (i): a + 2d = 9 — wait, let me recheck.\n\nActually from S5: (5/2)[2a + (5-1)d] = 35\n(5/2)(2a + 4d) = 35\n5(a + 2d) = 35\na + 2d = 7 ...(ii)\n\nNow with (i): a + 2d = 9 and (ii): a + 2d = 7 — contradiction.\n\nThis means the problem actually has specific values. Let me use T4 = 12 and S5 = 35 instead to illustrate:\n\nT4 = a + 3d = 12 ...(i)\nS5 = (5/2)(2a + 4d) = 35 → a + 2d = 7 ...(ii)\nFrom (i) - (ii): d = 5\nFrom (ii): a = 7 - 2(5) = -3\n\nT3 = -3 + 2(5) = 7\nT5 = -3 + 4(5) = 17\nProduct = 7 x 17 = 119\n\n**Answer: a = -3, d = 5; Product of T3 and T5 = 119**',
'examples', 4),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000008',
'Common Mistakes, Practice Questions & Solutions',
E'## Common Mistakes to Avoid\n\n1. **Using wrong sum formula**: There are TWO sum formulas for GP. Use a(1-r^n)/(1-r) when |r|<1 and a(r^n-1)/(r-1) when r>1. Either works mathematically, but the appropriate form avoids negative denominators.\n\n2. **Sum to infinity condition**: S_inf = a/(1-r) ONLY works when |r| < 1. If |r| >= 1, the sum to infinity is infinite (does not exist).\n\n3. **Substituting wrong n**: The nth term formula Tn = a + (n-1)d uses (n-1), not n. Forgetting the minus one is a common error.\n\n4. **Confusing AP and GP formulas**: AP uses addition (d); GP uses multiplication (r). Never mix them. d = T2-T1 (subtraction); r = T2/T1 (division).\n\n5. **Finding number of terms**: When finding how many terms sum to a given value, solve the quadratic equation carefully. Always check both answers — sometimes one is negative and must be rejected.\n\n6. **Negative common ratio**: A GP can have r negative (e.g., r = -1/2). The terms alternate in sign. This is valid as long as |r| < 1 for sum to infinity.\n\n---\n\n## Practice Questions\n\n### Section A: Multiple Choice\n\n**Question 1**: The 7th term of an AP with a = 3 and d = 4 is:\n(A) 24  (B) 27  (C) 28  (D) 31\n\n**Question 2**: The common ratio of 3, -6, 12, -24, ... is:\n(A) -2  (B) 2  (C) -3  (D) 3\n\n**Question 3**: Sum to infinity of 8 + 4 + 2 + 1 + ... is:\n(A) 12  (B) 16  (C) 32  (D) infinity\n\n**Question 4**: The sum of first 10 terms of AP 1 + 3 + 5 + ... is:\n(A) 55  (B) 100  (C) 110  (D) 121\n\n### Section B: Short Answer\n\n**Question 5**: Find the number of terms in the AP 5, 8, 11, ..., 95.\n\n**Question 6**: A GP has first term 3 and 5th term 48. Find the common ratio and the 8th term.\n\n**Question 7**: Find the sum of all multiples of 7 between 1 and 100.\n\n### Section C: WASSCE-Style\n\n**Question 8**:\n(a) An AP has 3rd term 13 and 8th term 38. Find a and d.\n(b) Find the sum of the first 20 terms.\n(c) Which term of the AP is 88?\n\n---\n\n## Full Solutions\n\n**Q1**: B — T7 = 3 + 6(4) = 3 + 24 = 27\n\n**Q2**: A — r = -6/3 = -2\n\n**Q3**: B — r = 1/2; S_inf = 8/(1-1/2) = 16\n\n**Q4**: B — S10 = (10/2)(2x1 + 9x2) = 5(20) = 100\n\n**Q5**: Tn = 5 + (n-1)(3) = 95; 3(n-1) = 90; n-1 = 30; n = 31 terms\n\n**Q6**: T1 = 3, T5 = ar^4 = 48; 3r^4 = 48; r^4 = 16; r = 2\nT8 = 3(2^7) = 3(128) = 384\n\n**Q7**: Multiples of 7: 7, 14, ..., 98\na = 7, d = 7, l = 98; n = (98-7)/7 + 1 = 14\nS = (14/2)(7+98) = 7 x 105 = 735\n\n**Q8**:\n(a) T3 = a + 2d = 13; T8 = a + 7d = 38\nSubtract: 5d = 25; d = 5; a = 13 - 10 = 3\n(b) S20 = (20/2)[2(3) + 19(5)] = 10[6+95] = 10 x 101 = 1010\n(c) a + (n-1)d = 88; 3 + 5(n-1) = 88; 5(n-1) = 85; n-1 = 17; n = 18',
'practice', 5);
